## 51 recorded 49: fact Transcript type=assistant uuid=39417725-278f-4f94-974c-3392bc309f4d
item stream:7c35f49e-797d-4a04-8177-c686eb905aa4 message final at=1793454884429 text="Rope-making is one of the oldest crafts in human history, with evidence stretching back tens of thousands of years. Archaeologists have found twisted plant fibers at sites such as Lubang Jeriji Saléh in Borneo, and fragments of cordage impressed into Paleolithic pottery and clay, suggesting that early people were spinning and plying fibers from grasses, bark, and sinew long before the invention of agriculture. Because natural fibers decay quickly, the surviving record is sparse, but impressions and a few preserved pieces show that the basic principle of twisting short fibers into a continuous strand was understood very early.\n\nOver time, rope became essential to nearly every society. Ancient Egyptian tomb paintings and surviving artifacts show ropes of papyrus and flax used for construction, rigging boats, and hauling stone for the pyramids. Mesopotamian, Chinese, and Andean cultures developed their own materials and methods, including hemp in China and cotton and agave fibers in the Americas. Andean peoples in particular produced remarkably strong suspension bridges from grass and plant cordage, some of which lasted for centuries and were maintained by communal labor.\n\nThe craft changed dramatically with the Industrial Revolution and the arrival of new materials. Machine spinning and laying replaced hand twisting in the 18th and 19th centuries, and manila and sisal fibers became major commercial sources for shipping. In the 20th century, synthetic fibers such as nylon, polyester, and polypropylene reshaped the field, offering greater strength, resistance to rot, and water durability. Traditional hand-laid rope is still made and valued today, but most modern rope is produced by factories using synthetic polymers."
