## 72 recorded 70: fact Transcript type=user uuid=3bd614e4-f5dd-4afd-8dab-8d23dbe0c7a7
item 3bd614e4-f5dd-4afd-8dab-8d23dbe0c7a7 prompt final at=1793454885061 text="Now two short paragraphs about knots."
snapshot WORKING queue=[] working_on=- at=1793454884928 asks=[] session=85327afd-9223-451a-b1cc-4b539831d962 model=claude-haiku-5-5 model_name="Haiku 5.5" effort=medium mode=default context=35584/? tasks=? active=[] usage=? servers=[] sign_in=? background=[]
## 76 recorded 74: fact Hook hook_event_name=MessageDisplay
item stream:0966ee93-522b-4deb-83ae-2c62a8df6583 message open at=1793454886624 text="Knots are almost as old as rope itself. Early humans tied fibers together to make nets, snares, baskets, and clothing, and the oldest knotted objects that survive are a few thousand years old, found in Egyptian and Peruvian contexts. Knot tying developed into a practical skill across cultures, with sailors, climbers, fishers, and craftspeople each building up their own repertoires. Many familiar knots, such as the bowline, the clove hitch, and the sheet bend, were refined through generations of use, and they were valued because they could be tied quickly, held under load, and untied when needed.\n\n"
## 78 recorded 76: fact Hook hook_event_name=MessageDisplay
append stream:0966ee93-522b-4deb-83ae-2c62a8df6583 "In the modern era, knots became the subject of systematic study. Sailors' knot manuals from the 19th and early 20th centuries catalogued and standardized them, and mathematicians later developed knot theory, which classifies knots by their topology and has applications in chemistry, biology, and physics. Today knots remain central to sailing, climbing, rescue work, and everyday life, and many of the same techniques used by ancient peoples are still taught and relied upon."
## 82 recorded 80: fact Transcript type=assistant uuid=9e28bb58-c37b-454e-88c0-15644850a063
item stream:0966ee93-522b-4deb-83ae-2c62a8df6583 message final at=1793454887718 text="Knots are almost as old as rope itself. Early humans tied fibers together to make nets, snares, baskets, and clothing, and the oldest knotted objects that survive are a few thousand years old, found in Egyptian and Peruvian contexts. Knot tying developed into a practical skill across cultures, with sailors, climbers, fishers, and craftspeople each building up their own repertoires. Many familiar knots, such as the bowline, the clove hitch, and the sheet bend, were refined through generations of use, and they were valued because they could be tied quickly, held under load, and untied when needed.\n\nIn the modern era, knots became the subject of systematic study. Sailors' knot manuals from the 19th and early 20th centuries catalogued and standardized them, and mathematicians later developed knot theory, which classifies knots by their topology and has applications in chemistry, biology, and physics. Today knots remain central to sailing, climbing, rescue work, and everyday life, and many of the same techniques used by ancient peoples are still taught and relied upon."
